# Rave: a design that is an application

A Rave document **is** an application — routes, layouts, pages and a stylesheet
with real breakpoints, drawn by the EVG engine — written as HTML-shaped markup
with no ids in it. When the work is "design a screen / a dashboard / an app" in
this repository, this is the thing to reach for rather than HTML or a picture.

The `ranger-design` MCP server (declared in `.cursor/mcp.json`) has the tools:

| tool | what it is for |
| --- | --- |
| `rave_spec` | the format in full. **Read it first**, once. |
| `rave_new` | a new application — `start`: dashboard, crud, marketing, masterdetail, settings, empty |
| `rave_read` / `rave_write` | the markup out and back. `rave_write` checks what it wrote |
| `rave_check` | **the loop.** Parse errors with lines, every CSS declaration the engine refused, every accessibility problem, per route per width |
| `rave_shot` | paint one route at one width and look at it |
| `figma_check` / `figma_markup` / `figma_tree` | a `.fig` file read as an application |

The same thing from a terminal: `npm run rave -- check app.rave`,
`npm run rave -- shot app.rave --width 390`, `npm run figma -- check app.fig`.

## The rules that are not negotiable here

- **Only class selectors.** `style="…"` on an element, or `<style class="x">…`.
- **`calc()`, `aspect-ratio`, `min-content`, `max-content`,
  `repeat(auto-fit, …)`, `box-shadow`, `transform`, `transition` and the
  per-side border shorthands do not exist.** A rule that uses one is dropped
  silently, and `rave_check` is the only place that says so.
- **Accessibility is checked, not suggested**: every input a `label`, every img
  an `alt`, every button words or a `label`, headings one level at a time,
  4.5:1 contrast.
- **Never finish on a `RAVE FAIL`.** Every line it prints is something a person
  would have found by looking.
